1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,150
Welcome to Class 14: from instrument to analysis for wet-lab teams.

2
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:09,363
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:09,363 --> 00:00:15,917
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:15,917 --> 00:00:18,257
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:18,257 --> 00:00:25,381
After this course, you should be able to: distinguish an instrument-control computer from storage and compute

6
00:00:25,381 --> 00:00:32,504
systems; explain how the Lab network limits Internet exposure while preserving approved server and proxy-based update

7
00:00:32,504 --> 00:00:38,291
access; choose among browser upload, SFTP, mounted storage, server-to-server transfer, and automated ingestion;

8
00:00:38,291 --> 00:00:44,079
estimate whether a dataset is difficult because of size, file count, or both;

9
00:00:44,079 --> 00:00:51,202
verify a transfer before deleting the source; explain why analysis should not run on an instrument-control

10
00:00:51,202 --> 00:00:58,325
computer; identify which data may be placed on RCC; place instrument data in an approved RCC

11
00:00:58,325 --> 00:01:05,003
project rather than a user's home directory; recognize and replace a saved Windows or macOS.

12
00:01:05,003 --> 00:01:10,562
### Instrument-control computer This system operates the sequencer, mass spectrometer, or microscope.

13
00:01:10,562 --> 00:01:17,047
Its priorities are stable acquisition, vendor support, enough local space for the current run,

14
00:01:17,047 --> 00:01:18,900
and minimal software change.

15
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:26,312
Do not use it as a general-purpose analysis workstation unless the instrument facility explicitly supports that

16
00:01:26,312 --> 00:01:26,776
use.

17
00:01:26,776 --> 00:01:32,335
Where suitable, ask RCC whether the device can join the Lab network.

18
00:01:32,335 --> 00:01:37,894
Do not connect it yourself or guess network, server, or proxy settings.

19
00:01:37,894 --> 00:01:44,379
RCC must first review the owner, software and update requirements, required direct server endpoints,

20
00:01:44,379 --> 00:01:48,085
vendor remote-support needs, data flow, and destination project.

21
00:01:48,085 --> 00:01:53,644
The Lab network is not an Internet connection with extra firewall rules..

22
00:01:53,644 --> 00:01:58,224
At every transition, somebody must know who owns the next step.

23
00:01:58,224 --> 00:02:03,637
This is the target lifecycle for data using the planned RCC-to-Coscine archive service.

24
00:02:03,637 --> 00:02:10,299
Coscine eligibility and the transfer route must still be confirmed for the specific project before real

25
00:02:10,299 --> 00:02:11,132
data moves.

26
00:02:11,132 --> 00:02:17,794
### The RCC landing point is a project, not a home directory Instrument data must land

27
00:02:17,794 --> 00:02:20,292
in the approved RCC project area.

28
00:02:20,292 --> 00:02:26,954
A user's home directory is for personal configuration, small source files, and individual working material—not authoritative

29
00:02:26,954 --> 00:02:29,452
research data or shared instrument output.

30
00:02:29,452 --> 00:02:36,114
This separation matters for governance and legal compliance: a project connects the data to an approved

31
00:02:36,114 --> 00:02:37,364
purpose, accountable owner.

32
00:02:37,364 --> 00:02:45,998
Record: project and responsible researcher; facility and instrument; run identifier; sample identifiers without direct patient identifiers;

33
00:02:45,998 --> 00:02:53,014
expected output size and file count; source directory and destination project; required analysis;

34
00:02:53,014 --> 00:02:57,332
retention requirements; person responsible for confirming the transfer.

35
00:02:57,332 --> 00:03:04,348
Use a directory name such as: Do not place patient names, birth dates,

36
00:03:04,348 --> 00:03:08,125
hospital numbers, or similar fields in filenames.

37
00:03:08,125 --> 00:03:13,543
Mounted storage is convenient but is not the preferred path for instrument datasets.

38
00:03:13,543 --> 00:03:21,474
A 500 GB dataset in five files can be easier to move than a 50 GB

39
00:03:21,474 --> 00:03:23,457
dataset in 500,000 files.

40
00:03:23,457 --> 00:03:31,388
Record total bytes, file count, largest file, directory depth, and whether files are still being written.

41
00:03:31,388 --> 00:03:39,319
Microscopy and mass-spectrometry workflows often create preview images, indexes, sidecars, databases, tiles, channels, or time-point files.

42
00:03:39,319 --> 00:03:46,755
Do not copy a live acquisition directory unless the facility has a tested ingestion process.

43
00:03:46,755 --> 00:03:54,734
RCC is appropriate for reproducible batch processing, many samples or images, CPU parallelism,

44
00:03:54,734 --> 00:04:02,099
GPU acceleration, large memory, containers, automated quality control, and durable project-level results.

45
00:04:02,099 --> 00:04:10,692
RCC is not automatically appropriate for live instrument control, diagnostic workflows, unsupported vendor databases,

46
00:04:10,692 --> 00:04:14,375
or data with primary identifying fields.

47
00:04:14,375 --> 00:04:22,118
The manifest should record relative path, size, role, sample ID, format, and creation time.

48
00:04:22,118 --> 00:04:25,989
Generate checksums only after acquisition is complete:

49
00:04:25,989 --> 00:04:31,299
Verify that: the destination exists; file count matches; total size is plausible;

50
00:04:31,299 --> 00:04:37,493
checksums match where practical; completion markers exist; key files open in the correct software;

51
00:04:37,493 --> 00:04:43,687
permissions allow the project team to read the data; source and destination are recorded.

52
00:04:43,687 --> 00:04:48,554
Only then should deletion be considered under the facility retention policy.

